From c4cd34f8f6bda0a6dde37837456d1f25c7c29be2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:08:26 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] Free the namespace copy -addNamespace: does not keep

-addNamespace: works on a copy of the namespace it is given.  When the
element already declares a namespace with the same prefix, the copy
replaces it only if that declaration is a placeholder; for a real one
the copy is linked nowhere and is never freed.  The element keeps its
first declaration, as before.

This has leaked since the method began copying (7e995ff2a, 2012), for
any prefix.  It surfaced in the test added by #820, which adds a second
default namespace to an element that already declares one: valgrind
reports the copy made at the start of -addNamespace: as definitely lost.

Free the copy and go on with the declaration the element has, so that
everything after the search refers to a namespace the element owns.

There is no test in the suite: the fault is a leak, which only a leak
checker sees.  Under valgrind, Tests/base/NSXMLNode/defaultNamespace.m
loses one block without the change and none with it; adding a
namespace twice, with an empty prefix and with a non-empty one, loses
96 bytes in two blocks without it and none with it.  The NSXMLNode,
NSXMLElement and NSXMLDocument tests are unaffected.
---
 Source/NSXMLElement.m | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/NSXMLElement.m b/Source/NSXMLElement.m
index b1f048532..9cb9b3627 100644
--- a/Source/NSXMLElement.m
+++ b/Source/NSXMLElement.m
@@ -564,6 +564,12 @@ - (void) addNamespace: (NSXMLNode*)aNamespace
           ns->next = cur->next;
           cur->next = NULL;
         }
+      else
+        {
+          xmlFreeNs(ns);
+          ns = cur;
+          prefix = ns->prefix;
+        }
     }
 
   // Are we setting a default namespace?
